WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:08.080
Paramount closed its $110 billion takeover of&nbsp;
Warner Bros… when it was worth… $11 billion?

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.160
The question is… how?

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.400
How is it that a company can&nbsp;
acquire a giant, seven times bigger?

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:20.080
The mechanics of how this all played out,&nbsp;
are crazy, but also, happening more often.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:21.440
Something is changing.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.080
You no longer need to dilute shares or&nbsp;
have lots of cash on hand to buy a giant.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.400
You just need one important thing.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:41.680
Which is how Paramount acquired&nbsp;
Warner Bros… and made them pay for it.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:47.440
Now Paramount is not a small company…&nbsp;
But it is much smaller than Warner Bros.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.120
At the time of the deal, Paramount&nbsp;
was worth about $11 billion.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:56.960
Warner Bros Discovery, was worth&nbsp;
$77.71B. About seven times larger.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.080
To find out how, we need to go&nbsp;
back, to when the architect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:03.840
this entire merger first took over&nbsp;
Paramount, just a few years ago.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:09.120
Some companies have two classes of&nbsp;
shares. Class A, and Class B. Typically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.080
one of them has most of the votes. In&nbsp;
the case of Paramount, it’s Class A.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:19.200
Which means, you don’t need to own&nbsp;
the whole company… or even a majority,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.040
just enough voting power.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:26.560
Paramount was controlled by another company,&nbsp;
National Amusements, owned by the Redstone family.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:32.800
National Amusements held 77% of Paramount's&nbsp;
votes… and just 10% of the company.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.000
Which is exactly how one man&nbsp;
seized control of Paramount,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.920
for… not $11 billion, but just $2.4 billion.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:41.280
David Ellison.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:44.240
You might recognize that name, “Ellison”.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.600
That’s because he's the son… of Larry Ellison.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:49.920
Co-Founder and Chairman of Oracle.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.840
Larry was, at least briefly, the&nbsp;
richest man on earth last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.360
after announcing a deal between Oracle and OpenAI.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.360
He’s currently #8, and this&nbsp;
will be very important later.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:07.600
But by 2025, David Ellison's group, by acquiring&nbsp;
National Amusements, now controlled Paramount.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.000
And this would set the stage for the massive&nbsp;
acquisition just a couple years later.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.440
But, the Warner Bros merger… wasn’t done this way.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:20.720
Paramount, wanted the entire company, and&nbsp;
the way that was done, was even stranger.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:25.680
It would take a lot more money, a lot more&nbsp;
partners, and have a lot more opposition.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.040
On September 11th, 2025, the&nbsp;
Wall Street Journal reported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:32.000
that Paramount was preparing to buy Warner Bros.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:37.280
This caused the Warner shares to jump 30%,&nbsp;
to $40 billion, compared to Paramount’s $19B.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:42.000
Sure enough, later that month,&nbsp;
Paramount makes its first offer:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.840
$19 a share, part cash, part Paramount shares.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.240
This is a pretty standard acquisition…

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:50.880
…and the Warner Bros board rejects it.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:56.560
Paramount makes a second offer. $22 a share,&nbsp;
plus $2 billion if regulators block the deal.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.120
The Warner Bros board rejects it.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.760
Paramount offers $23.50 a share.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.080
...The Warner Bros board rejects it.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:08.000
From here, things get a bit crazy,&nbsp;
and I think the Ellisons realized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.000
they’d need to flex more financial&nbsp;
muscle to conquer Warner Bros.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.720
Business ideas don’t die because they’re&nbsp;
bad. It’s not really about the idea at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.280
but turning from an idea, into action.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:20.760
And one of the best ways t&nbsp;

