00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.840
WBD's board puts the company up for sale,&nbsp;
since, there was clearly a lot of interest.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:02.960
An auction ensues, but it wasn’t&nbsp;
Paramount who won. It was Netflix.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:09.200
Netflix would buy the studios, HBO,&nbsp;
and HBO Max for $27.75 a share,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:14.560
$82.7B including debt. A cash&nbsp;
and stock deal, like Paramount.

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:18.640
A public company traditionally pays&nbsp;
for an acquisition in one of two ways:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:22.960
with its own shares, or with cash&nbsp;
borrowed against its own earnings.

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:25.680
Both limit it to targets near its own size.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:28.480
Netflix, fit your typical acquisition description.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:33.760
A stock and cash deal, from a much bigger&nbsp;
company, with lots of revenue, and deep pockets.

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:37.440
But, an Ant can’t acquire&nbsp;
a Giant, with a stock deal.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.720
But Paramount could no&nbsp;
longer afford a stock offer.

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:45.200
And to understand why, let’s&nbsp;
briefly look at another acquisition.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:51.520
In September 2001, HP announced it would buy&nbsp;
Compaq for $25 billion, entirely in stock.

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:57.280
HP’s shareholders voted, 51.4%&nbsp;
for and 48.6% against, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:00.000
son of the HP co-founder pushed back against it.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.560
HP shares began to fall, and so did&nbsp;
the purchase price. To $21 billion.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:06.240
Then to $19 billion.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:08.880
HP and Compaq were roughly the same size,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:14.080
but just imagine how many shares Paramount&nbsp;
would need to issue, to compete with Netflix.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:20.320
Paramount’s own shares were worth only about&nbsp;
$19B, and just $14 billion at the start of 2026.

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:24.160
The existing owners would be&nbsp;
diluted into… basically nothing.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:27.520
If you pay in your own shares for&nbsp;
a company seven times your size,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:30.560
Warner Bros, would end up&nbsp;
owning most of the merger.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:37.520
And on hand, Paramount had just $3.3&nbsp;
billion… not to mention $13 billion of debt.

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:40.960
But David Ellison, wanted&nbsp;
control of the whole thing.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:45.200
So where the heck did they&nbsp;
find around $80 billion?

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:47.920
Well Paramount took a different approach.

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:52.720
And here, we need to talk about two&nbsp;
secret weapons they brought to the table.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:02.880
Paramount decided to launch&nbsp;
a “hostile tender offer”.

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:08.640
If the board keeps rejecting you, go to the people&nbsp;
who actually own the company. The shareholders.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:10.800
Offer them the money directly.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:13.520
But, Paramount needed to get that money first.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:18.240
So the first thing Paramount did, which&nbsp;
isn’t anything new, is raise debt.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:23.120
$54B of loans, committed from&nbsp;
Bank of America, Citi, and Apollo.

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:24.960
That’s a lot of debt…

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:30.720
And as we’ll soon see, I think this might be&nbsp;
the most ironic part of the whole takeover.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:36.000
Nonetheless, this still wasn’t enough, which&nbsp;
brings us to Paramount’s first secret weapon.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:37.120
Ellison wealth.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:40.160
David Ellison had his father’s&nbsp;
wealth at his disposal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:43.520
and this was a large part of&nbsp;
how they funded the takeover.

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:48.240
This was less a Paramount acquisition,&nbsp;
and more of an Ellison acquisition.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:50.720
But this came with a huge problem.

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:54.800
Larry Ellison also backed the deal way&nbsp;
back in September, during the first bid.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:57.840
But Warner Bros, didn’t see it as a good offer.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:00.400
In the board’s letter to shareholders, they said:

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:05.280
“[Paramount] has consistently misled WBD&nbsp;
shareholders that its proposed transaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:11.680
has a ‘full backstop’ from the Ellison family.&nbsp;
It does not, and never has. The Ellisons were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:16.400
relying on an unknown and opaque revocable&nbsp;
trust for the certainty of this crucial deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:22.960
funding. A revocable trust is no replacement for a&nbsp;
secured commitment by a controlling stockholder.”

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:25.040
And the board was right.

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:29.440
Larry Ellison’s wealth is in a revocable&nbsp;
trust. A revocable trust can be changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:34.880
or emptied by its owner at any time, and&nbsp;
could disappear before the deal is finished.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:38.320
So, Paramount launches their hostile tender offer.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:40.800
$30 a share in cash for all of WBD.

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:43.840
$18B more than Netflix’s.

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:46.720
Paramount also raises the&nbsp;
fee it would pay Warner Bros,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:49.840
if regulators blocked the deal, to $5.8 billion.

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:54.400
Larry Ellison also gave an “irrevocable&nbsp;
personal guarantee of $40.4 billion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:58.560
the equity financing”, and agreed not&nbsp;
to revoke the Ellison family trust.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:02.720
But, this was still just in&nbsp;
a trust, not with Paramount.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:05.360
Then, Paramount does something interesting.

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:11.520
The Ellison family trust commits up to&nbsp;
$46.72B to buy brand-new Paramount shares&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:17.600
at $16.02 each. Specifically, Class&nbsp;
B stock, which has no voting rights.

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:22.560
That’s 2.9B new shares, and&nbsp;
a 60% premium to the market.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:25.360
But isn’t this diluting ownership?

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:30.160
Well yes, but not for the Ellisons.&nbsp;
They still hold the voting power.

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:33.200
But it wasn’t just the&nbsp;
Ellisons funding this takeover.

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:38.080
The commitment was “up to $46 billion&nbsp;
of new shares” and some of those shares,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:41.520
$24 billion worth, were&nbsp;
being bought by someone else.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:43.920
Paramount’s second secret weapon.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:45.280
Sovereign wealth funds.

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:49.600
Saudi Arabia's PIF, an Abu Dhabi fund&nbsp;
(L'imad), the Qatar Investment Authority,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:52.400
and LionTree, a New York investment firm.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:55.040
And this is becoming far more common.

00:09:55.040 --> 00:10:01.280
By 2026, sovereign wealth funds passed $15&nbsp;
trillion in assets for the first time. In 2025,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:05.440
there was $278 billion across 562 investments.

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:10.800
The recent takeover of Electronic Arts, for&nbsp;
example, was done largely by Saudi Arabia's PIF.

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:14.320
The three Gulf funds would&nbsp;
invest about $24 billion.

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:17.440
But the stranger part, is that…&nbsp;
these wealth funds would hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:20.560
zero voting power in the Paramount Warner merger.

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:24.080
The Ellisons would own the&nbsp;
vast majority of voting rights.

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:27.440
But, things got even more tense from here.

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:33.200
The board says “[Paramount] is a company with a&nbsp;
$14 billion market capitalization attempting an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:39.520
acquisition requiring $94.65 billion of debt&nbsp;
and equity financing, nearly seven times its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:44.320
total market capitalization. This aggressive&nbsp;
transaction structure poses materially more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:50.160
risk for WBD and its shareholders when compared to&nbsp;
the conventional structure of the Netflix merger.”

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:56.400
Netflix, changes to an all-cash deal, which for&nbsp;
the size of the offer, was pretty impressive.

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:01.360
But, if we look back to the HP Compaq&nbsp;
example, we can understand why. Their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:05.920
share price, was falling, weakening their&nbsp;
earlier offer, which included stock.

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:09.760
Paramount seems to have an edge,&nbsp;
and they do something crazy.

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:15.760
They raise their price to $31 a share, but&nbsp;
also essentially pay Netflix, to go away.

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:21.137
Paramount offers to pay the $2.8B&nbsp;
breakup fee WBD owes Netflix.

00:11:21.137 --> 00:11:21.200
They also offer to pay $7 billion&nbsp;
if regulators block the deal.

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:23.520
And, they add a “ticking fee”.

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:28.240
Every quarter the deal is delayed, they&nbsp;
would raise the price $0.25 a share.

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:31.200
They were removing Warner Bros’ reasons to say no.

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:35.200
Of course, they couldn’t remove the&nbsp;
biggest reason, which we’ll get to soon.

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:37.440
But, this had swayed things a lot.

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:43.040
The board called this deal “superior” to&nbsp;
Netflix’s, who doesn’t match. Netflix co-CEO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:49.360
Ted Sarandos calls Paramount’s bid "unusual"&nbsp;
and "irrational", and, he isn’t wrong.

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:55.760
But on February 27, 2026, Paramount and&nbsp;
Warner Bros Discovery sign a merger agreement:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.760 --> 00:12:00.080
$31 a share in cash, $80.9B for the shares.

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:03.200
The largest all-cash deal in corporate history.

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:06.400
The merged company will be called Skydance.

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:10.480
And there’s a huge, glaring&nbsp;
problem with all of this.

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:12.000
You might have picked up on it.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:12.768
Debt.

00:12:12.768 --> 00:12:12.800
Debt, debt, a whole lot of debt.

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:16.640
This is the strangest, most&nbsp;
ironic part of this whole merger.

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:30.160
And how, in the end, the one truly paying&nbsp;
for this entire thing… is Warner Bros.

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:36.800
The total cash cost of the merger was $80.9B,&nbsp;
but that’s just for the shares. The total value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:41.440
of the deal was actually $110 billion,&nbsp;
because of Warner Bros’ existing debt.

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:44.960
Warner Bros, had just gone through&nbsp;
another merger a few years ago.

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:49.680
In 2021, WarnerMedia was owned by&nbsp;
AT&amp;T, after their acquisition in 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:53.120
when they acquired Time Warner for $85.4 billion.

00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:57.360
AT&amp;T then spun off WarnerMedia, which&nbsp;
is what they renamed the company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:01.920
into Discovery, to form “Warner Bros&nbsp;
Discovery”, for $43 billion in cash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:06.240
while AT&amp;T shareholders still&nbsp;
owned 71% of the new company.

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:10.240
Obviously, this left Warner&nbsp;
Bros, in a pretty bad spot.

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:13.600
Debt from all these different&nbsp;
takeovers and mergers piled up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:18.320
and the company ended 2025 with $29 billion of it.

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:22.160
It was worth about $25 billion in early 2025,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:28.640
not too much more than Paramount. But after the&nbsp;
bidding war, its value had grown 3.2x, up $56B.

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:34.640
Though, even if the value skyrocketed,&nbsp;
it was still money Paramount had to find.

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:37.440
Which brings us to why I think this is so ironic.

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:40.160
Paramount had $15 billion of debt.

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:42.640
Warner Bros Discovery had about $30 billion.

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:46.320
And Paramount borrowed $52&nbsp;
billion to finance the deal!

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:51.600
Which left the new company with…&nbsp;
wait for it… $80 billion in debt.

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:54.480
7.6 times their combined earnings.

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:59.360
And Skydance, which in large part&nbsp;
is Warner Bros, has to pay off.

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:04.160
So, in a way, the one who really has&nbsp;
to pay for the merger, is Warner Bros.

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:08.400
The question now, is how the heck&nbsp;
are they going to pay this off?

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:11.440
This is the ongoing question,&nbsp;
and the pressure now sitting on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:17.200
Skydance’s Chairman and CEO David Ellison&nbsp;
and the newly appointed co-CEO Ynon Kreiz.

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:20.560
They’ve promised more than $6&nbsp;
billion a year in cost savings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:25.760
within 3 years of closing the deal. Which&nbsp;
they say will come from “non-labor sources”.

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:30.960
Ted Sarandos, from Netflix, believes&nbsp;
"there'll be cuts in excess of $16 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:33.840
